      {
        "finding_id": "section-bad1a41a755ef2ded5cc",
        "file": "ournavybarbaryco02alle_ournavybarbaryco02alle_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Our navy and the Barbary corsairs",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/ournavybarbaryco02alle",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": [
            "Allen, Gardner Weld, 1856-1944"
          ],
          "date": [
            "1905-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "1135a3a79197e1c82225ecc0079d7260b2a1d27346d5b5129e0575b8a3277b07",
        "evidence_excerpt": "Leaving  Naples  August  3,  the  squadron  arrived  at \nLeghorn  on  the  13th.  Here  the  commodore  learned \nthat  the  corsairs  of  Algiers  and  Tunis  M^ere  in  port, \nand  so  no  longer  to  be  feared.  The  Enterprise  was \nsent  to  Malta  for  dispatches  said  to  have  been  for- \nwarded there ;  the  John  Adams  was  sent  with  convoy \nto  Gibraltar,  and  the  Adams  to  Tunis  with  Consul \nCathcart,  and  thence  to  Gibraltar.  Cathcart  had  in- \nstructions from  the  secretary  of  state,  dated  April  9, \n1803,  to  offer  the  bey  of  Tunis,  but  only  if  it  seemed \nnecessary  for  the  preservation  of  peace,  tribute  not \nexceeding  ten  thousand  dollars  a  year,  to  be  paid \nbiennially  in  cash  and  not  in  naval  stores.  The  bey's \ndemand  for  a  frigate  was  to  be  refused.  The  siun  of \ntwenty  thousand  dollars  was  allowed  for  a  peace  with \nTripoli,  to  be  followed  by  the  same  periodical  pay- \nments as  in  the  case  of  Tunis.  This  was  to  be  a  pri- \nvate arrangement  and  not  incorporated  in  the  treaty. \nThe  secretary  of  state  believed  that  tribute  would  be \nnecessary  to  secure  peace  with  the  pasha  of  Tripoli,",
        "years_in_scope": [],
