      {
        "finding_id": "section-ade85de15221a27da0ac",
        "file": "ournavybarbaryco02alle_ournavybarbaryco02alle_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Our navy and the Barbary corsairs",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/ournavybarbaryco02alle",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": [
            "Allen, Gardner Weld, 1856-1944"
          ],
          "date": [
            "1905-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "1135a3a79197e1c82225ecc0079d7260b2a1d27346d5b5129e0575b8a3277b07",
        "evidence_excerpt": "vessels  and  gunboats  from  America,  when  the  entire \nsquadron  might  have  been  made  ready  for  united  ac- \ntion against  the  enemy.  The  mere  assembling  of  such \na  formidable  force  before  Tripoli  might  have  fright- \nened the  pasha  into  yielding  any  terms  demanded, \nespecially  if  Eaton's  movement  on  land  had  been  re- \ninforced and  pushed.  As  it  was,  after  Preble  with- \ndrew his  force,  there  never  appeared  before  Tripoli, \nat  any  one  time,  more  than  a  few  blockading  vessels, \nand  the  real  strength  of  the  squadron  was  never \ndemonstrated  to  the  pasha.  The  national  honor  was \nvastly  more  important  than  hastening  the  release  of \nthe  captives  in  Tripoli,  and  the  nation,  having  out- \ngrown the  weakness  and  poverty  of  the  earlier  years, \nshould  have  been  above  haggling  with  a  pirate.",
