      {
        "finding_id": "section-bfbc5a95c8aec39230ee",
        "file": "seawolvesofmedit00curruoft_seawolvesofmedit00curruoft_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Sea-wolves of the Mediterranean : the grand period of the Moslem corsairs",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/seawolvesofmedit00curruoft",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": "Currey, Edward Hamilton",
          "date": [
            "1913-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8ded5d2596f6b8b5da24b9515a2ae1cca80e39c88e17cfd932f28761d003774e",
        "evidence_excerpt": "Consequently,  greatly  daring,  he  sent  an  embassy \nto  the  Sublime  Porte  with  one  of  his  most  trusted \ncaptains  at  its  head  to  lay  the  homage  of  the  cor- \nsairs at  the  feet  of  Selim  I.  Very  naturally  these \nambassadors  did  not  go  empty-handed,  but  took \nwith  them  rich  presents  and  numerous  slaves. \nSelim  was  much  pleased  at  the  attention,  coming  as \nit  did  from  such  a  distance — we  have  to  remember \nthat  the  coast  of  North  Africa  was  an  immense \njourney  from  Constantinople  in  those  days — and \nthe  insight  of  Kheyr-ed-Din  was  triumphantly \nvindicated.  Not  only  did  the  Sultan  send  a  gracious \nreply  in  return,  but — what  was  far  more  to  the \npurpose — he  sent  a  reinforcement  of  fourteen \nvessels  to  the  corsairs,  bidding  them  to  go  on  and \nprosper  in  their  efforts  to  spread  the  true  faith \namong  the  Christian  heretics.",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
