      {
        "finding_id": "section-6daf6e57272a4d88d364",
        "file": "seawolvesofmedit00curruoft_seawolvesofmedit00curruoft_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Sea-wolves of the Mediterranean : the grand period of the Moslem corsairs",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/seawolvesofmedit00curruoft",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": "Currey, Edward Hamilton",
          "date": [
            "1913-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8ded5d2596f6b8b5da24b9515a2ae1cca80e39c88e17cfd932f28761d003774e",
        "evidence_excerpt": "eyes  on  this  remnant  of  the  power  of  Spain  in \nAfrica.  Could  he  but  dislodge  Martin  de  Vargas, \nhe  had  the  whole  of  Northern  Africa  practically  at \nhis  disposal ;  Algiers  would  then  be  really  his,  to \nfortify  for  all  time  against  the  inroads  of  his  foes. \nHe  was  master  by  land  and  sea,  the  time  was  pro- \npitious ;  the  corsair  decided  that  the  hour  had  come. \nHe  had  seen  the  repulse  of  his  brother  Uruj,  none \nknew  better  than  did  he  the  temper  of  the  men  by \nwhom  the  Pefion  was  held,  or  the  valiance  and  the \nunswerving  fidelity  of  that  caballero  of  Spain, \nMartin  de  Vargas.  He  tried  to  induce  that  officer \nto  surrender  to  him,  offering  every  inducement  to  the \nSpanish  commander  to  come  to  terms.  He  was  met \nwith  a  haughty  refusal,  couched  in  the  most  con- \ntemptuous language.  He  tried  the  most  blood- \ncurdling threats,  which  were  no  empty  menaces,  as \nhis  adversary  well  knew  :  these  were  received  in \nsilence.",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
