      {
        "finding_id": "section-d89d79367105821995e8",
        "file": "seawolvesofmedit00curruoft_seawolvesofmedit00curruoft_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Sea-wolves of the Mediterranean : the grand period of the Moslem corsairs",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/seawolvesofmedit00curruoft",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": "Currey, Edward Hamilton",
          "date": [
            "1913-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8ded5d2596f6b8b5da24b9515a2ae1cca80e39c88e17cfd932f28761d003774e",
        "evidence_excerpt": "Even  those  hardened  men  of  blood  shrank  before \nthe  horror  which  was  proposed  to  them  by  their \nchief,  and  Sinan-Reis  took  up  his  parable  and  spoke \nthe  minds  of  all  when  he  said  that  follow  him  to  the \ndeath  they  would  cheerfully  do,  but  stain  them- \nselves with  so  awful  a  massacre  was  to  place  them- \nselves outside  the  pale  of  humanity  for  ever.  It \nwas  seldom  that  they  crossed  his  mood,  and  Bar- \nbarossa listened  in  frowning  silence,  accepting \nas  a  partial  excuse  that  time  pressed,  and  to  put \nto  .  death  twenty  thousand  persons  would  occupy \nlonger  time  than  they  could  spare.  On  the  morrow \na  battle  was  fought,  which,  as  Kheyr-ed-Din  anti- \ncipated, ended  in  the  complete  rout  of  the  Moslems. \nEverywhere  the  Corsair  King  was  in  the  forefront \nof  the  battle,  and  it  is  said  that  he  disposed  of",
