      {
        "finding_id": "section-e8771a34503675383295",
        "file": "seawolvesofmedit00curruoft_seawolvesofmedit00curruoft_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Sea-wolves of the Mediterranean : the grand period of the Moslem corsairs",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/seawolvesofmedit00curruoft",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": "Currey, Edward Hamilton",
          "date": [
            "1913-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8ded5d2596f6b8b5da24b9515a2ae1cca80e39c88e17cfd932f28761d003774e",
        "evidence_excerpt": "The  council  of  the  town  of  \"  Africa  \"  troubled \nthemselves  but  little  more  concerning  Dragut,  his \nships,  and  his  corsairs  ;  he  had  departed,  and  as  the \ndays  wore  on  and  no  further  tidings  of  him  came  to \nhand,  these  simple  folk  thanked  God  that  they  were \nrid  of  a  knave  and  went  about  their  usual  avocations \nas  unconcernedly  as  if  no  sea-wolves  lurked  under \nthe  shadowed  headlands  of  that  continent  in  which \ntheir  homes  were  situated.  They  were  a  people \nessentially  of  the  land ;  although  they  dwelt  on  the \nconfines  of  the  ocean  the  ways  and  habits  of  those  who \nearned  a  precarious  living  on  the  waters  were  a \nsealed  book  to  them,  and  with  the  \"  Africans  \"  it  was \na  case  of  \"  out  of  sight  out  of  mind  \"  so  far  as  the \ncorsairs  were  concerned.  But  that  black-hearted",
