      {
        "finding_id": "section-77c7ac14d142eb7a6ee7",
        "file": "seawolvesofmedit00curruoft_seawolvesofmedit00curruoft_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Sea-wolves of the Mediterranean : the grand period of the Moslem corsairs",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/seawolvesofmedit00curruoft",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": "Currey, Edward Hamilton",
          "date": [
            "1913-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8ded5d2596f6b8b5da24b9515a2ae1cca80e39c88e17cfd932f28761d003774e",
        "evidence_excerpt": "In  the  choking  heat  of  the  African  night  towns- \nmen and  corsairs  wrestled  in  deadly  conflict  hand  to \nhand  and  foot  to  foot ;  but  these  untrained  lands- \nmen stood  but  a  poor  chance  against  the  picked \nfighting  men  of  the  Moslem  galleys  who  had  been \ninured  to  bloodshed  from  their  earliest  youth  and \ntrained  by  such  a  master  in  the  art  of  war  as  Dragut. \nThat  warrior,  his  great  curved  scimitar  red  to  the  hilt, \nthe  blood  dripping  from  a  gash  in  his  cheek,  his \nclothing  torn  and  in  disarray,  followed  by  a  gigantic \nnegro  bearing  a  flaming  torch,  was  ever  in  the \nthickest  of  the  fray.  Behind  him  his  lieutenants \nOthman  and  Selim  strove  to  emulate  his  prowess, \nwhile  all  around  surged  his  devoted  band  of  fanatics.",
