      {
        "finding_id": "section-c87589bc93fcbc709352",
        "file": "seawolvesofmedit00curruoft_seawolvesofmedit00curruoft_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Sea-wolves of the Mediterranean : the grand period of the Moslem corsairs",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/seawolvesofmedit00curruoft",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": "Currey, Edward Hamilton",
          "date": [
            "1913-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8ded5d2596f6b8b5da24b9515a2ae1cca80e39c88e17cfd932f28761d003774e",
        "evidence_excerpt": "With  the  remainder  of  the  siege,  which  was  yet \nto  last  till  September  i8th,  we  have  no  concern  in \nthis  book.  It  is  only  necessary  to  say  that  the  men \nof  II  Borgo  were  worthy  to  stand  in  the  same \ncategory  with  the  defenders  of  St.  Elmo,  which  is \nequivalent  to  stating  that  in  them  also  was  dis- \ncovered the  last  limit  of  heroism.  The  Grand \nMaster  survived  the  siege,  his  monument  is  the \nnoble  city  of  \"  Valetta  \"  built  on  Mount  Sceberras. \nThe  Turks  abandoned  the  siege  and  returned  to \nConstantinople  on  the  arrival  of  some  insignificant \nreinforcements  from  Sicily.  So  terrible  had  been \nthe  resistance  of  the  Knights  that  no  heart  was  left \nin  their  armada.  Of  Dragut  there  remains  but \nlittle  to  be  said :  he  was  perhaps  the  best  educated \nof  the  corsairs  and  less  cruel  than  was  usually  their \nhabit.  Although  not  so  renowned  as  his  more",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
