      {
        "finding_id": "section-5a0b46d14135dea6d3f7",
        "file": "studiesinnorthaf0000gran_studiesinnorthaf0000gran_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Studies in North Africa",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/studiesinnorthaf0000gran",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": "Grant, Cyril Fletcher",
          "date": [
            "1923-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "75673ef6cd5c3422a56d91532eed41c92aa6b4d4c68a81636004012e2f399b15",
        "evidence_excerpt": "Practically, however, Kheir-ed-Din’s fame rests \nupon the fact that he was the founder of Algiers \nas a corsair stronghold, the chosen home of the \nworst desperadoes in the world. Yet at first the \nrulers of Algiers were not, in the full sense of the \nword, pirates. They fought the Holy War against \ninfidels but they did it as subjects of Turkey; \nthey respected Powers which were at peace with \ntheir suzerain, and the government was, nominally \nat least, in the hands of a Pacha appointed by the \nSultan. But it was a far ery to Constantinople; \nthe links with Turkey rapidly grew weaker and \nweaker, and the real power passed into the hands \nof the soldiery, who revived the glories of the old \nPretorian Guard of Rome, making and unmaking \nrulers at their pleasure, removing any unpopular \nPacha by assassination, and keeping the real \npower in the hands of their own commander or \nAgha. At first he was given only the title of",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
