        "source_text_sha256": "6138c424ee238e85fb6fb48ba525f1c29c7f976cf104138f88baec22a14297cd",
        "evidence_excerpt": "Weak links. The brothers’ chronicle is a panegyric of uncertain authorship: its 1837 \neditors could not identify its author, dated it as contemporary because it speaks of \nKhayr al-Din's son Hasan as living, and suspected an official commission; modern \nscholarship connects it to the Turkish Gazavdt composed for Khayr al-Din by Seyyid \nMurad, a connection not examined here. Its speeches are literary compositions and are \nused for the position they describe, not as a record of words spoken. Haedo compresses \nNavarro’s taking of Oran and of Bougie into the single year 1509; the authorship of the \nTopographia is a question this study has not examined. Leo's \"Selim Etteumi\" and \nHaedo's \"Selim Eunttemi” are the same man under two spellings. Drops. Haedo's own \nsentence describing Aruj killing Selim al-Tumi \"con sus manos\" in a bath was dropped, \nbecause the page check matched a different occurrence of the words and could not be \ncompleted; Leo's verified sentence carries the event instead. Leo's statement that the \ntownsmen \"began to play the pirates\" was dropped as a quotation, since it falls across a \npage break the check did not cover, and is paraphrased. A reported inscription of April \n1520 styling Khayr al-Din \"sultan of the warriors” was not used: it is known here only \nfrom a modern summary, and Devoulx's Epigraphie indigéne (1874), where it may be \nprinted, could not be downloaded in this session. It belongs, if confirmed, to the",
        "years_in_scope": [],
