      {
        "finding_id": "section-2849fb01cca226aa1510",
        "file": "the-regency-of-algiers_The_Regency_of_Algiers_djvu.txt",
        "title": "The Regency Of Algiers",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/the-regency-of-algiers",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": "Thomas J. Bryan, Claude/Anthropic",
          "date": [
            "2026-09-16T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "6138c424ee238e85fb6fb48ba525f1c29c7f976cf104138f88baec22a14297cd",
        "evidence_excerpt": "What followed is recorded by Haedo in a single parenthesis, set aside for fuller \ntreatment elsewhere in his book: \"Lo que despues Barbarroja hizo del Capitan Martin \nde Vargas (y quan barbara y cruelmente le mato en su presencia a palos, sin causa alguna \no razon, al cabo de tres meses que era captiuo)\" — what Barbarossa afterwards did with \nCaptain Martin de Vargas, and how barbarously and cruelly he had him beaten to \ndeath in his presence with staves, for no cause or reason, after he had been three \nmonths a captive. The editors of 1837, following the chronicle's side, tell a different \nstory of those months, in which Khayr al-Din at first honoured his prisoner and \ngranted his request that the Spanish deserter who had revealed the fort's hunger be \nflogged and beheaded, before turning on Vargas himself. Both accounts end in the \nsame death. Haedo adds that Barbarossa then had the fort pulled down, and modern \naccounts add that its stone went into the mole that made the harbour — so that the \nanchorage from which the corsairs of the next three centuries sailed was built, in the \nmost literal sense, out of the fortress that had forbidden them the sea.",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "EXPLICIT_PARAGRAPH",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
