      {
        "finding_id": "section-e36905e2fda4d5b0bfc5",
        "file": "the-regency-of-algiers_The_Regency_of_Algiers_djvu.txt",
        "title": "The Regency Of Algiers",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/the-regency-of-algiers",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": "Thomas J. Bryan, Claude/Anthropic",
          "date": [
            "2026-09-16T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "6138c424ee238e85fb6fb48ba525f1c29c7f976cf104138f88baec22a14297cd",
        "evidence_excerpt": "The janissaries, he says, were not always in Algiers; winter and summer they went \nout through the kingdom in companies of four, five and six hundred, as the king \nrequired and the agha ordered, to collect the tributes of the Moors and Arabs by force \nof arms, because otherwise, unless compelled, they would not pay; the companies \nstayed out four or five months at a time, and others went out as they came in. Some of \nthe janissaries preferred to sail with the corsairs, \"pero muchos, y los mas huelgan de yr \nen las Mahales a garramar, que es sin duda para ellos vna manera de rico corso, y de \nrobar por la tierra, porque demas de que comen siempre por do van de lo que toman y \nroban a los moros y alarbes, los desuellan, por fuerca les toman\" — but many, and \nmost, delight in going out with the columns to collect, which is without doubt for \nthem a kind of rich corsairing and robbing by land, because besides always eating \nwherever they go from what they take and rob from the Moors and Arabs, they flay",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
