          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "6138c424ee238e85fb6fb48ba525f1c29c7f976cf104138f88baec22a14297cd",
        "evidence_excerpt": "The second is what the corps was made of, and it corrects the common picture in a \nspecific way. The janissary name crossed the sea and left behind the institution that \nmade janissaries a sultan's household — the levy of Christian boys, the palace, the lack \nof any bond but the master. What Algiers had instead was a stream of adult volunteers \nfrom Anatolia, admitted by a concession Khayr al-Din obtained, and later corsairs and \nrenegades admitted by a settlement between two armed corporations that joined them \nby sharing their incomes. A corps built that way could have no loyalty except to its pay, \nand its pay was its own.",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
          "url": "https://archive.org/details/the-regency-of-algiers",
