        },
        "source_text_sha256": "6138c424ee238e85fb6fb48ba525f1c29c7f976cf104138f88baec22a14297cd",
        "evidence_excerpt": "The register's structure is itself a statement of title. The state took its eighth before \nanything else, and the ruler chose his captives first. The costs of the port and of the \nofficials who handled the prize came next, each a small share of the flow attached to an \noffice. Then what remained was cut in half, and one half was divided into shares among \nthe men who had sailed, according to their rank, while the other half went to whoever \nowned the ship. A note at the head of the register, datable to 1797 and added to over \nthe following years, lists the ships of the corsair fleet with the number of shares each \ncarried, and records a refinement that was later abandoned: \"Ces parts, dites grandes \nparts, étaient prélevées en dehors des parts ordinaires, et allouées au capitaine et 4 \nquelques officiers. Plus tard ce systéme fut aboli.\" These shares, called great shares, were \nlevied outside the ordinary shares and allotted to the captain and some officers; later \nthis system was abolished.",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "EXPLICIT_PARAGRAPH",
        "date_evidence": null,
