        "source_text_sha256": "6138c424ee238e85fb6fb48ba525f1c29c7f976cf104138f88baec22a14297cd",
        "evidence_excerpt": "The Laugier account describes the office in the 1720s under the name of the \nPitremelgi. \"Upon the Death of any one, having no Child or Brother, the Pitremelgi \nseizes his whole Substance, paying the Widow her Jointure.\" He had the houses of the \ndead man searched, in town and country, for hidden gold and silver, and the account \nexplains why men hid it. If a private person was reported to be rich, \"the Dey certainly \ncasts his Eye upon him, exacting Money under Pretence of the Necessities of the State, \nor for any frivolous Trespass. And sometimes confiscates his whole Estate, both real and \npersonal, on a forged Suspicion of having engaged in a Conspiracy.\" Rich men, it \ncontinues, therefore preferred to be thought poor and buried their treasure against the \nday they might have to flee.",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "EXPLICIT_PARAGRAPH",
        "date_evidence": null,
