        "source_text_sha256": "6138c424ee238e85fb6fb48ba525f1c29c7f976cf104138f88baec22a14297cd",
        "evidence_excerpt": "The first test concerned the recruiting ground itself. Devoulx, commenting on an \ninscription of the dey Hajji Ali (1809-1815), records that during a long and \nunsuccessful war with Tunis the dey put himself in open rebellion against the sultan \nand defied him by seizing ships crewed by Greeks who were Ottoman subjects, and that \nthe Porte threatened war and \"s'opposait au recrutement de la milice algérienne,\" \nopposed the recruitment of the Algerian militia, which weakened the corps \nconsiderably. Hajji Ali was strangled in his bath by a servant the conspirators had won \nover. The lever the Porte held was not a fleet but a door: the Anatolian ports through \nwhich the corps renewed itself.",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "EXPLICIT_PARAGRAPH",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
