        "source_text_sha256": "6138c424ee238e85fb6fb48ba525f1c29c7f976cf104138f88baec22a14297cd",
        "evidence_excerpt": "For a man of a subject tribe, the cost was the column itself, and its credibility \ndepended on distance. The companies that went out every season to collect from the \ntribes — which Haedo described, as a Spanish churchman, as a rich corsairing by land, \nliving off what they took and taking by force what was not given — came to the plains \naround the capital and the provincial towns reliably and regularly. For a tribe within \ntheir reach, refusal meant the column's return with the makhzan cavalry beside it, and \ncompliance bought the column's departure. What compliance did not buy was \nanything the tribesman valued positively from the order, beyond being left alone until \nthe next season.",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
