        "source_text_sha256": "6138c424ee238e85fb6fb48ba525f1c29c7f976cf104138f88baec22a14297cd",
        "evidence_excerpt": "And last, what I expected and did not find. I expected corsairing to be the Regency's \nmain revenue at every period, because that is what the word pzvate state promises. In \nthe one year whose accounts survive it contributed nothing; the register shows it large \nand volatile in the war years between 1793 and 1815 and small in others; and at every \nperiod reached here the order also lived on tribute that was the price of not being \nraided, on monopolies that taxed its own producers through prices, on the property of \nthe dead, and on the unpaid labour of those it held. The corsair was the loudest \nmechanism. He was not the only one, and in the last years of the order he was not there",
