        "source_text_sha256": "6138c424ee238e85fb6fb48ba525f1c29c7f976cf104138f88baec22a14297cd",
        "evidence_excerpt": "Quoted exactly, and how verified. Pierre Dan, Histoire de Barbarie et de ses \ncorsaires (2nd ed., Paris, 1649; archive.org histoiredebarbarOOdanp): the \npassages on Sidi Utica, the marabout of Tlemcen's promise that the enemy's muskets \nwould not fire, the flaying of thirty-two principal men and the parade of their skins, the \ndivan's use of Turkish and its interpreter, the \"bruit & vn hurlement estrange,\" the man \nwho raises his crossed hands and is thrown into the sea, and the cry \"CHARALA,” \nwere read from images of the printed page; long s is printed as s, and the renderings are \nmine. The cry of \"Charala\" is quoted from Dan's account of women at the gate of the \nKasba in a separate episode; his statement that women petitioning the divan were \nwillingly heard is paraphrased from the text layer. Venture de Paradis, \"Alger au XVII]e \nsiecle,” Revue africaine 40 (1896): the divan no longer consulted and punishment \"4 \nhuis clos,\" confirmed by independent OCR of cropped lines. Shaler, Sketches of Algiers \n(1826): the divan as \"a dead letter,” Omar Dey’s refusal to write in any language but his \nown, the dragoman who \"cannot with safety be relied on,” the consul’s newspapers",
        "years_in_scope": [
          1649
        ],
