      {
        "finding_id": "section-019a912593a48d1bd967",
        "file": "the-regency-of-algiers_The_Regency_of_Algiers_djvu.txt",
        "title": "The Regency Of Algiers",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/the-regency-of-algiers",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": "Thomas J. Bryan, Claude/Anthropic",
          "date": [
            "2026-09-16T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "6138c424ee238e85fb6fb48ba525f1c29c7f976cf104138f88baec22a14297cd",
        "evidence_excerpt": "For an officer of standing — a senior soldier with a following, a minister, a captain \n— the order offered one prize seniority could not deliver, and \"every Turk ... being \nqualified to rise to the Deylik\" meant that any of them might take it. The conspiracies \nthe Laugier account describes did not threaten the arrangement; they were one of its \nworking parts. A successful cabal killed a man and kept everything else: the register, the \npay scale, the offices, the sultan's investiture, which the new dey would ask for as his \npredecessor had. A failed cabal's leaders were strangled or driven out, and the register \nwas untouched. Plotting against the dey was the ambitious officer's way of defending \nthe arrangement while trying to rise within it.",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
          "url": "https://archive.org/details/the-regency-of-algiers",
          "source_file": "the-regency-of-algiers_The_Regency_of_Algiers_djvu.txt",
