          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "6138c424ee238e85fb6fb48ba525f1c29c7f976cf104138f88baec22a14297cd",
        "evidence_excerpt": "The fourth is where fracture could come from. Two groups with standing and \nmeans had interests that did not run with the register: the beys, whose offices were \nbeginning to pass from father to son and who commanded their own cavalry and \nrevenue; and, after 1816, the corsair captains, whose heritable capital had lost its \npurpose. A third, the kouloughlis, had property, kin and a grievance, and were kept out \nof the Kasba. The men of religion and the makhzan chiefs would survive any master. \nAnd under all of them lay the one condition on which the soldiers’ loyalty to the \narrangement depended: that the chest in the Kasba could pay the register. In the one \nyear whose accounts survive, it was paying more than it received, from a hoard that \ncould not be refilled.",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "EXPLICIT_PARAGRAPH",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
