      {
        "finding_id": "section-5075890c9869fb89987b",
        "file": "the-regency-of-algiers_The_Regency_of_Algiers_djvu.txt",
        "title": "The Regency Of Algiers",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/the-regency-of-algiers",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": "Thomas J. Bryan, Claude/Anthropic",
          "date": [
            "2026-09-16T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "6138c424ee238e85fb6fb48ba525f1c29c7f976cf104138f88baec22a14297cd",
        "evidence_excerpt": "Its war at sea was the war every Mediterranean power waged: licensed, adjudicated, \nrecorded in a register with a published share table, conducted under a flag and limited \nby treaty — the same war the Knights of Malta waged from the other shore under the \ncross. Its captives could be ransomed, and converts among them rose to command. \nAnd the powers that called it a nest of pirates and slavers were in the same centuries \ncarrying Africans across the Atlantic on a scale the Regency never approached: the \nmodern Trans-Atlantic Slave Trade Database reckons some twelve and a half million \nembarked between 1501 and 1866, more than three million of them under the British \nflag, most in the century before a British fleet bombarded Algiers in the name of \nabolition. When the Regency fell, it fell to a foreign army, not to its own people, and",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "EXPLICIT_PARAGRAPH",
