      {
        "finding_id": "section-928013cb6ca786f7111a",
        "file": "trent_0116401962679_96_trent_0116401962679_96_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Journal of a voyage into the Mediterranean",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/trent_0116401962679_96",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": "Digby, Kenelm, 1603-1665",
          "date": [
            "1868-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "b29ea2f541355e88a69fafd370e61db2820d23f3707cedbf6ac8626a9e948c7b",
        "evidence_excerpt": "On  his  trial,  Sir  Everard  petitioned  with  earnestness  that  his \npunishment  might  not  extend  beyond  himself.  He  craved  especially \nthat  his  wife  might  enjoy  her  jointure,  and  his  son  the  benefit  of  an \nentail  made  long  before  any  thought  was  entertained  of  the  Gun¬ \npowder  Conspiracy.  Attorney- General  Coke  answered  “  Nimrod \nDigby  ”  with  needless,  but  with  him  (it  is  to  be  feared)  customary, \nferocity,  that,  inasmuch  as  for  the  Catholic  cause  he  had  been  content \nto  neglect  the  ruin  of  himself,  his  wife,  his  estates,  “  and  all/’  he \nshould  have  his  desire  “as  ’tis  in  the  Psalm,  ‘Let  his  wife  be  a \nwidow,  and  his  children  vagabonds,  let  his  posterity  be  destroyed, \nand  in  the  next  generation  let  his  name  be  quite  put  out.’  ”  The \nanathema  of  the  Hebrew  poet,  no  less  than  the  law  of  the  Attorney- \nGeneral,  failed  in  its  application  to  the  family  of  Sir  Everard. \nAfter  a  litigation  of  several  years,  the  law  upheld  the  conveyance \nto  uses  which  he  had  made,  and  thus  lands  stated  to  have  been  of \nthe  value  of  3,000£.  per  annum*  were  saved  to  his  family,  f",
